course: dictionary
lesson: 8886
title: Showmanship
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Showmanship is skill at entertaining a crowd and getting attention. A performer with showmanship does things in a bold, exciting or dramatic way, so people want to watch. Think of a footballer doing a trick with the ball, or a magician swirling a big cape.
explain: Showmanship is a noun that you cannot count, so you say «a bit of showmanship» or «pure showmanship», not "showmanships". The clue in «monks give demonstrations with hard training and some showmanship» is that the training is real, and the showmanship is the exciting way it is shown to the crowd.
explain: Showmanship can be praise or criticism. When a performer makes a show fun to watch, it is praise. When someone shows off more than they are good, it is criticism. Ask yourself: is the writer pleased or annoyed? The words around the word tell you which.
fact: Showmanship means skill at entertaining a crowd and getting attention.
fact: «Pure showmanship» means a performance that is all about being exciting to watch.
fact: The Shaolin Temple in Henan province, about 1,500 years old, is famous for kung fu.
fact: In the example, the monks' skill is real, but nobody flies: that part is film magic.
fact: Showmanship is an uncountable noun, so it has no plural.
exercise: «The magician swept his big cape over the table with great showmanship.» What does showmanship mean here? | *Skill at entertaining a crowd in a bold, exciting way | Being very tired after a long show | Shouting at the audience | A list of tricks written down
exercise: «The monks train hard, but nobody flies: that is film magic.» Why does the writer say nobody flies? | The writer is a magician | Flying is easy for monks | Monks cannot train hard | *Flying is not real, so the film made it look like magic
exercise: Ava says, «He had a lot of showmanship, but he only wanted to show off.» Is Ava praising him or criticising him? | Saying he is shy | Saying he cannot dance | *Criticising him, because he cared more about showing off than being good | Praising him for being very quiet
test: «The footballer did a trick with the ball, pure showmanship.» What clue shows that showmanship means exciting entertainment? | *The trick was done to entertain the crowd | The ball was flat | The crowd was asleep
test: Which phrase uses showmanship correctly? | many showmanships | more showmanship than the other showmanships | showmanships of the cape | *a bit of showmanship
exam: Ruby says, «Her showmanship lit up the stage, but she showed off more than she sang well.» What does Ruby mean? | Praise only, for her singing | *Praise for the entertainment, with a hint of criticism for showing off | She was not on the stage | Criticism only, for losing her voice
